{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he took.</b> We do not know whether Gershom was born in Moses' youth and when he fled to Midian, or in his old age. Do not rely on the chronicles of Moses, for everything written in them is worthless. His second son is Eliezer, born now at the time of his prophetic calling. He intended to bring his wife and sons to Egypt, so that they would go out together with Israel — but this was not a sound plan, for he was coming to bring Israel out, and they would see that he came with his wife and sons to settle there. Do not wonder how a prophet might think something incorrect in worldly matters, for we find David who asked Nathan the prophet whether it was proper to build a house for HaShem, and he replied, \"All that is in your heart, do, for YHWH is with you,\" and at night it was said to him in prophecy that he should tell David that he would not build the house. They [the Sages] needed to translate על החמור as \"upon a human bearer,\" because it would be an undignified mode for the prophet's wife to ride on a single donkey, she and her two sons. Japheth said that it is the name of the species, like \"I had ox and donkey\" (Genesis 32:6), but he did not speak correctly, for this is not a place to mention the species name; rather it is as it sounds. And donkeys in Egypt are more expensive and esteemed than mules. Do not wonder if Gershom his son rode behind her — perhaps he was small — and Eliezer was in her lap, for he was not yet circumcised." "The sense of \"and he returned to the land of Egypt\" is that he alone returned, for when HaShem encountered Moses, Eliezer was circumcised, and when he healed, Zipporah returned with her sons to her father, and Moses left them and returned to Egypt." "He said concerning the staff \"the staff of Elohim\" on account of the sign that was performed with it. The staff of Elohim is Moses' staff, and it is Aaron's staff, for it was in his hand when he performed the signs with it. He now returns to explain — because he mentioned \"the staff of Elohim\" — what HaShem said to Moses: all the portents he was destined to perform in Egypt. And He made known to him the sign by which they would go out from Egypt, namely the plague of the firstborn." "Now \"and it came to pass on the way, at the lodging place\" should properly have been joined to \"and Moses took his wife and his sons,\" and many such instances occur in the Torah, such as \"he shall surely pay; if he has nothing\" (below 22:2), which is joined to \"five oxen for the ox\" (ibid. 21:37)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:43.136334352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}